  <notes>
    <summary>
      Built the shared, config-driven WizardFollowOns module ALONGSIDE the four
      existing wizards' local Next-Steps/SendEmail copies (no deletions — that is
      tasks 021-024, one wizard at a time so each migration is independently
      regression-testable). All 3 acceptance criteria verified; both quality
      gates (code-review + adr-check) clean — 0 Critical, 0 Warnings, 0 ADR
      violations.
    </summary>

    <abstraction-design>
      FollowOnCardConfig = { id, label, icon, description, renderStep } plus four
      OPTIONAL fields that absorb the divergence across the 4 future consumers
      without any fork:
        - `stepLabel` — card label vs sidebar label can differ.
        - `canAdvance` — required-field gate (Send Email requires To/Subject/Body)
          vs always-optional (Assign Work / Create Event). Defaults to always-true.
        - `isSkippable` — per-step Skip button.
        - `renderSelectedExtra` — inline grid UI shown only while a card is
          selected. This is the key fit for SummarizeFilesWizard, whose Send Email
          card carries an "include only short summary" toggle IN THE GRID. Without
          this slot, task 024 would have had to fork the grid. `id` is a plain
          `string` (not limited to the canonical FollowOnId union), so
          SummarizeFiles' non-canonical cards (create-project, work-on-analysis)
          are just config too.
      Why the four consumers fit: (Matter) assign-counsel/draft-summary/send-email,
      (WorkAssignment) assign-work/send-email/create-event, (CreateRecord/new
      wizards) assign-work/add-todo/send-email, (Summarize) send-email +
      create-project + work-on-analysis with the inline toggle — every difference
      is expressible as FollowOnCardConfig fields, none needs a grid variant.

      Non-obvious correctness decision: the grid drives addDynamicStep/
      removeDynamicStep IMPERATIVELY in the card click handler, NOT in a
      useEffect diff on `selected`. The grid is the "Next Steps" step CONTENT and
      unmounts whenever the user navigates onto an injected follow-on step; a
      mount-scoped effect would risk cleanup removing steps on unmount. Driving at
      click time (grid guaranteed mounted) is robust and keeps the grid a pure,
      testable function of its props. Canonical sidebar order defaults to the
      card-array order (a wizard controls ordering by ordering its array);
      FOLLOW_ON_CANONICAL_ORDER is exported as a convenience default.

      AddTodoFollowOnStep (net-new): the STEP is a thin controlled wrapper reusing
      the shared CreateTodoStep form (§11 reuse-first); the CREATE path is the
      exported `createTodoRegardingChild(dataService, formValues, child)` helper
      the wizard calls in onFinish. It delegates to the ADR-024-compliant
      TodoService.createTodo, seeding regarding to the JUST-CREATED CHILD (not the
      host) via the shared applyResolverFields primitive — no duplicated resolver
      logic. Known limitation surfaced (not a blocker): sprk_kpiassessment is not
      in TODO_REGARDING_CATALOG, so a To Do cannot be regarding a KPI record;
      createTodo returns success:false gracefully (test-covered). Task 023/024
      decides whether KPI offers the Add-To-Do card or the catalog is extended.
    </abstraction-design>

    <files-touched>
      NEW: src/client/shared/Spaarke.UI.Components/src/components/WizardFollowOns/
        followOnTypes.ts, FollowOnGrid.tsx, index.ts,
